Findings (2)

  HIGH    ES005 Expensive nested-loop inner side
          Nested Loop repeats Index Scan using orders_created_at_idx on orders 20 times; that takes
          100% of the loop's time.
          Inner loops: 20
          Inner side per loop: 15.6 ms
          Repeated work: 312.5 ms of the loop's 312.6 ms, 312.3 ms of it in the inner scans
          Rows removed per loop: 55,201 by the filter of Index Scan using orders_created_at_idx on
          orders
          → An index on orders (customer_id) would turn each of the 20 inner scans into an index
          lookup.

  HIGH    ES006 Index scan that filters most rows
          Index Scan using orders_created_at_idx on orders finds 1,104,080 rows through
          orders_created_at_idx and its filter throws away 1,104,020.
          Index: orders_created_at_idx
          Filter: (orders.customer_id = c.id)
          Rows removed by the filter: 55,201 of 55,204 found per loop
          Loops: 20
          → An index on orders that starts with customer_id and continues with the columns of
          orders_created_at_idx would let the index do the filtering.

Advice (1)

  LIKELY  Index orders (customer_id, created_at)
          CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ON public.orders (customer_id, created_at);
          An index on orders (customer_id, created_at) would turn each of the 20 runs of Index Scan
          using orders_created_at_idx on orders into an index lookup.
          ! Not connected to the database: existing indexes, the table's write load and the column
          statistics were not checked.
          ! created_at is taken from the name of orders_created_at_idx, which Index Scan using
          orders_created_at_idx on orders reads in order; plans do not list index columns.

